/*
------------------------------------------------------------
Problem    : Combination Sum II
Platform   : LeetCode
Topic      : Backtracking
Difficulty : Medium

Problem:
Given a collection of candidate numbers and a target value,
find all unique combinations whose elements sum to the target.

Each element can be used only once.

The result must not contain duplicate combinations.

Approach:
Use Backtracking along with sorting.

First, sort the candidates so that duplicate values become
adjacent.

During backtracking, start from the current index and try
each available candidate.

After choosing a candidate, recursively continue from
`index + 1` because each element can only be used once.

To avoid duplicate combinations, skip a candidate when it is
the same as the previous candidate at the same recursion
level.

Why does this work?

Sorting places duplicate values next to each other.

For example:

[1,1,2,5]

If we are at the same recursion level and have already
considered the first `1`, choosing the second `1` as the
starting choice would generate duplicate combinations.

Therefore:

if (i > start && candidates[i] == candidates[i - 1])

we skip the duplicate.

However, duplicate values can still be used in the same
combination when they occur at different recursion levels.

For example, [1,1,6] is valid.

Algorithm:
1. Sort the candidates.
2. Start backtracking from index 0.
3. If the remaining target becomes zero, store the combination.
4. Skip duplicate candidates at the same recursion level.
5. Choose the current candidate.
6. Recursively continue from `i + 1`.
7. Remove the candidate after returning from recursion.
8. Stop the loop when the candidate exceeds the target.

Time Complexity : O(2^n)

Reason:
Each element can be either selected or skipped, giving up
to 2^n possible subsets in the worst case.

Sorting additionally takes O(n log n).

Space Complexity : O(n)

Reason:
The recursion depth and current combination can contain at
most n elements, excluding the output space.

------------------------------------------------------------
*/

class Solution {
private:

    // Generates all unique combinations using backtracking.
    void backtrack(
        vector<int>& candidates,
        int remainingTarget,
        int startIndex,
        vector<int>& currentCombination,
        vector<vector<int>>& result
    ) {

        // A valid combination has been found.
        if (remainingTarget == 0) {
            result.push_back(currentCombination);
            return;
        }

        for (int index = startIndex;
             index < candidates.size();
             index++) {

            // Skip duplicate values at the same recursion level.
            if (index > startIndex &&
                candidates[index] == candidates[index - 1]) {
                continue;
            }

            // Since the array is sorted, all later values
            // will also be greater than the remaining target.
            if (candidates[index] > remainingTarget) {
                break;
            }

            // Choose the current candidate.
            currentCombination.push_back(candidates[index]);

            // Move to index + 1 because each element
            // can only be used once.
            backtrack(
                candidates,
                remainingTarget - candidates[index],
                index + 1,
                currentCombination,
                result
            );

            // Undo the choice.
            currentCombination.pop_back();
        }
    }

public:
    vector<vector<int>> combinationSum2(
        vector<int>& candidates,
        int target
    ) {

        // Sorting allows duplicate values to be detected easily.
        sort(candidates.begin(), candidates.end());

        vector<vector<int>> result;
        vector<int> currentCombination;

        backtrack(
            candidates,
            target,
            0,
            currentCombination,
            result
        );

        return result;
    }
};
